<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/policy-pointers/">Policy pointers</a><span>/</span>How to Read the Insurer’s Estimate, Line by Line</nav><span class="kicker fade-up">Guide · 7 min read · August 25, 2026</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.08s">How to Read the Insurer’s Estimate, Line by Line</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.15s">A guided tour of a typical property claim estimate: the summary, line items, depreciation, O&amp;P and what to look for when something seems missing.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Start with the summary page</h2>
<p>The summary shows replacement cost (RCV), depreciation, actual cash value (ACV), deductible and net payment, usually by coverage. Check that the deductible matches your declarations page and that depreciation is marked recoverable if you have replacement cost coverage.</p>
<h2>Then the line items</h2>
<p>Each line has a description, quantity, unit, unit price and total. Look at:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Quantities.</strong> Do roof squares, wall areas and flooring match the real dimensions? Use our <a href="/oregon-claim-calculators/roof-pitch-squares-converter/">roof pitch and squares converter</a> for roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Remove and replace.</strong> Is there a line to tear out and dispose of damaged material, not just install new?</li>
<li><strong>Prep and protection.</strong> Masking, floor protection, content manipulation and cleanup.</li>
<li><strong>Trades.</strong> Is every trade needed listed, such as electrician, plumber and painter?</li></ul>
<h2>Depreciation</h2>
<p>Look for how depreciation was calculated, whether labor was depreciated, and whether any items were treated as non-recoverable. The <a href="/oregon-claim-calculators/depreciation-holdback-tracker/">depreciation and holdback tracker</a> shows the effect.</p>
<h2>Overhead and profit</h2>
<p>When a repair needs several trades coordinated by a general contractor, estimates often include overhead and profit. If yours doesn’t, ask why.</p>
<h2>Commonly missing items</h2>
<ul><li>Matching of adjacent finishes</li><li>Code-required items</li><li>Steep and high roof charges</li><li>Minimum charges for small trades</li><li>Permits and dumpsters</li></ul>
<p>If you find a meaningful gap, a <a href="/property-appraisals/carrier-estimate-review/">professional estimate review</a> can confirm it before you supplement or demand appraisal.</p>
